
{"id":"a5b788f2-ee31-40d8-8af3-f1037602b932","object":"chat.completion","created":1784999694,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"","reasoning_content":"We need to parse the task. The task is SE-PE-01, described as \"grammar_guided_node_reduction\" with a bounded objective. The input case has nodes with parent, id, symbol, depth, removable, utility. Also required_symbols. We need to output removed_node_ids and retained_node_ids.\n\nThe algorithm: \"Consider deeper parse-tree nodes before shallower nodes. Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children. Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented. Use node ID as the stable tie break at equal depth. Return sorted retained IDs and the actual removal order.\"\n\nSo it's like Perses reduction: we have a tree, we want to remove removable nodes in order, but only if removing them doesn't cause removal of required symbols or violate interestingness (but there's no explicit interestingness function given; maybe it's simply that required symbols must remain? The description says \"interestingness oracle\" but not defined; maybe it's simply that required_symbols must be present in the retained set? Also \"grammar_valid\" might be that after removal, the tree structure must be valid: i.e., each node that is retained must have its parent retained? Actually the tree might be a tree of parse nodes; if you remove a node, its children get attached to its parent? In Perses, removal of a node means deleting that node and attaching its children to its parent. But the description says \"Never remove a node with retained children\" – that suggests we can't remove a node if it has children that are retained. So that implies that to remove a node, all its children must also be removed (or perhaps they become the parent's children? But \"retained children\" means children that are not removed, so if we want to remove a node, we must first remove its children, or we cannot remove it if any child is retained. The rule says \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" So if a node has any child that is not removed, we cannot remove it. That implies we remove nodes from leaves upward. But the order is \"deeper nodes before shallower nodes\". So we process nodes in reverse depth order (deepest first). For each removable node, we check if removing it would cause any required symbol to be lost (i.e., that symbol appears only in that node's subtree and nowhere else in the retained tree). And also that the node has no retained children (which would be the case if we process in reverse depth, children are processed before parents, so if children are removable and we choose to remove them, they become removed, then the parent may have no retained children. But children might be non-removable (like root or required symbol). In that case, if a node has a non-removable child, we cannot remove it. So the algorithm: collect all nodes. Determine which nodes are removable (field removable true). Also nodes that are required symbols (symbol in required_symbols) cannot be removed (but they might not be marked removable anyway, but we should enforce). Root is not removable.\n\nWe need to decide the order of processing: \"Consider deeper parse-tree nodes before shallower nodes.\" So we need a list of removable nodes sorted by depth descending, and for equal depth, by node ID in a stable tie break (presumably alphabetical/numerical? ID is string, so we can use string comparison as tie breaker). Then we iterate through that list. For each candidate node, we check if it can be removed: it must be removable by its own flag (already in list). Also: it must not be a required symbol (i.e., symbol in required_symbols). Also: it must have no retained children. But \"retained children\" are children that are not removed yet. Since we are processing in depth order, children are deeper and will be considered before the parent. However, a child might be non-removable (e.g., required symbol, or removable but we haven't decided to remove it? Actually the decision is per node: we tentatively remove it if conditions are met. But if the child is removable but we choose not to remove it (because it would break interestingness?), then the parent might still have a retained child. But the algorithm as described says \"tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented.\" That means we need to check after removal, would any required symbol be missing from the tree? But the tree is not fully defined after removal: if we remove a node, its children are presumably also removed? Or do they get attached to parent? The description says \"Reduce a registered parse tree through grammar-valid deletion and replacement candidates\". In Perses, reduction often deletes a node and attaches its children to its parent. But the rule \"Never remove a node with retained children\" suggests that children are not automatically retained; they are removed if the parent is removed? Actually if you delete a node, you replace it with its children. So the children become part of the parent's children. That would mean children are retained even if the parent is removed. But then \"node with retained children\" would mean if any child is retained (i.e., not also removed), you cannot remove the node because that would cause the children to become orphans? No, the standard algorithm in Perses: you can delete a node and its children are promoted to be children of its parent. That's called \"deletion\" in the original paper. But the description here says \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" That implies if children are retained, you cannot remove the node. So the algorithm might be: we can remove a node only if all its children are also removed (i.e., the whole subtree is removed). That would be \"delete subtree\" rather than \"delete node and promote children\". Which one is it? Let's analyze the provided examples to infer.\n\nIn the first case (a-000):\nnodes: root (depth0, not removable), n1 (depth1, removable false), n2 (depth1, removable true), n3 (depth1, removable false), n4 (depth2, removable true, parent n2), n5 (depth2, removable false, parent n3), n6 (depth2, removable true, parent n1).\nrequired_symbols: root, s1.\nWhich nodes have symbol s1? n1 (symbol s1), n4 (symbol s1). So at least n1 and n4 represent s1.\nIf we remove n2, it has child n4 (removable true). n4 is symbol s1. If we remove n2, do we also remove n4? According to \"node with retained children\", if we remove n2, we must not have retained children, so n4 must be removed first. But n4 is removable and also has symbol s1. If we remove n4, then s1 would only be present in n1, which is non-removable, so it's okay. So we could remove n4 first, then remove n2. But n3 is non-removable, so n5 (depth2, non-removable) stays. n6 (depth2, removable) parent n1 (non-removable). n6 is removable and not required symbol (symbol s0). Can we remove n6? It has no children, and its symbol is not required. Removing n6 would not affect s1 or root. So n6 should be removed. Then we can consider n2? After removing n4 and n6, what about n2? n2's only child is n4 (removed), so n2 has no retained children, and it's removable. But symbol s2? required_symbols are root and s1, so s2 is not required. So we can remove n2. n1 and n3 are non-removable. So retained nodes: root, n1, n3, n5. That's 4 nodes. But what about the output? We don't have expected output for this fixture, but we can hypothesize. Also note that n5 is non-removable, so it stays. So removed nodes would be n4, n6, n2. Order: deeper first: n4 (depth2) and n6 (depth2) before n2 (depth1). at depth2, n4 and n6. Tie break: ID \"n4\", \"n6\". n4 < n6? string comparison \"n4\" < \"n6\" because '4' < '6', so n4 first, then n6, then n2. So removed_node_ids = [\"n4\",\"n6\",\"n2\"], retained_node_ids = [\"n1\",\"n3\",\"n5\",\"root\"] sorted alphabetically? The output requires retained sorted, removal order as actual order. So retained sorted: [\"n1\",\"n3\",\"n5\",\"root\"] and removed as [\"n4\",\"n6\",\"n2\"].\n\nNow look at the second case a-001: similar structure.\nnodes: root, n1 (removable false, s1), n2 (removable true, s2), n3 (removable false, s0), n4 (removable true, s1, parent n2), n5 (removable false, s2, parent n3), n6 (removable true, s0, parent n1).\nrequired: root, s1.\nSame reasoning: s1 appears in n1 and n4. n4 is removable, n1 non-removable. So we can remove n4 (providing s1 remains with n1). n6 is removable and not required. n2 is removable, has child n4 (if removed then no retained children). So we can remove n4, n6, n2. Same result? But note utilities differ, but not used. So output likely similar. \n\nThird case a-002: same structure.\n\nSo the algorithm is: remove removable nodes in reverse depth order, but only if the node is not required (symbol in required_symbols) and has no retained children (i.e., all children are either already removed or are non-removable? Actually \"retained children\" means children that are being kept. Since we process depth order, we decide removal per node. We must ensure that when we consider a node, all its children have already been processed (since deeper). For each child, if the child is retained (i.e., we decided not to remove it), then the node cannot be removed. If the child is removed, then it's not retained. So the condition is: node.removable is True, node's symbol not in required_symbols, and for every child, either the child is not retained (i.e., it's removed) or the child is non-removable? Wait, if a child is non-removable, it will always be retained. So a node with a non-removable child cannot be removed. Also if a child is removable but we choose not to remove it (maybe because it's required?), then it would be retained. But the decision to remove a child depends on that child's own conditions. For a leaf child (no children), if it's removable and not required, we will remove it. If it's removable but required, we cannot remove it. So the parent removal condition must consider the actual retained status of children after processing.\n\nSo the algorithm: Build a tree structure with children list. Identify root (parent null). Build a set of required symbols. Mark nodes that are non-removable: root, nodes with symbol in required_symbols, and nodes where removable is False. But note: a node with removable=False is non-removable. However, a node with removable=True but symbol in required_symbols is still non-removable. Also root is always non-removable (even if removable field says true? but in fixtures root's removable is false). So we compute a set of initially non-removable nodes.\n\nThen we process all nodes in order of decreasing depth, and for same depth, by node ID string (alphabetical order, as tie breaker). We'll have a list of candidate nodes (those that are removable and not already non-removable by above). But we need to consider that a node's removability can change based on children? No, once non-removable (required symbol, or non-removable flag, or root), it's always non-removable. But for other nodes, they are removable initially. However, we may decide not to remove a node if it has retained children that are non-removable or not removed. So we need to simulate removal decisions.\n\nThus we iterate through candidate nodes in order (deepest first). For each node, we check:\n- Is it still present (i.e., not already removed)? We'll maintain a set of removed nodes.\n- Is it non-removable? (but we already filtered, so it's removable)\n- Does it have any child that is not removed (i.e., retained)? If yes, cannot remove.\n- Does removing it cause any required symbol to be missing from the tree? That is, after removal, would any required symbol be absent? Since we haven't removed other nodes, we need to check the union of symbols of all retained nodes (including ancestors and descendants) still contain all required symbols. But careful: removing a node might remove the only occurrence of a required symbol if that symbol is only present in that node's subtree and all nodes in that subtree are removed. But if the node has required symbol itself, it would be non-removable already. However, if the node does not have the required symbol but its subtree contains the required symbol, removing the node would remove its subtree (since children are all removed? Actually if the node is removed, its children are also removed because we are deleting the whole subtree? The description says \"Never remove a node with retained children\", meaning if we remove a node, its children are not retained; they must be removed first. So removing a node implies its entire subtree is removed (since children must be removed before). So removing a node means we delete it and all its descendants. Therefore, we must ensure that no required symbol exists in that node's entire subtree. So we need to check if any required symbol is present in the subtree of node. If yes, we cannot remove.\n\nThus condition: node's subtree (including itself) contains any required symbol? Actually if node's own symbol is required, it's non-removable already. But if its descendant has a required symbol, that descendant is likely non-removable (since required symbol nodes are non-removable). But a required symbol node is non-removable, so it would be retained. But if we remove the node (parent), that would cause the required child to become orphan? Actually if we remove the parent, we are removing the whole subtree, so the required child would be removed as well. That is not allowed. So we must ensure that no required symbol node exists in the subtree. Since required symbol nodes are non-removable, they will not be removed from the set. But if we try to remove their ancestor, we would be removing them, which is invalid. So we must check: for the candidate node, is there any node in its subtree (including itself) that has a symbol in required_symbols? If yes, cannot remove.\n\nAlternatively, simpler: for each node, we can precompute the set of required symbols present in its subtree (including itself). Then if that set is non-empty, the node cannot be removed. But careful: a required symbol node might be removable? In fixtures, nodes with required symbol are marked removable false. But we should not rely on that; we must treat any node with symbol in required_symbols as non-removable regardless of removable flag. So precompute required subtree set.\n\nSo algorithm steps:\n1. Build a dict id->node object.\n2. Build children lists.\n3. Identify root (parent null).\n4. Compute required_set = set(required_symbols).\n5. For each node, compute the set of symbols in its subtree (including itself) that are in required_set. Use DFS post-order.\n6. Find all nodes that are \"removable_candidate\": \n   - node is not root\n   - node's removable field is True (but note: could be True even if symbol in required? but if symbol in required, we should treat as non-removable anyway, so we can filter out those)\n   - node's symbol not in required_set (so it's not required)\n   - node's subtree required_intersection is empty (no required symbol in subtree)\n   (But we also must consider that children might be non-removable for other reasons, like removable=False but not required. If a child is non-removable, then the parent cannot be removed because it would lose that child. So we need to ensure that all children of the node are either removable and will be removed (i.e., they are also in the removal candidate list and will be removed before) or are non-removable (and thus cannot be removed). But if a child is non-removable, then parent cannot be removed. So we need to check that for every child in the subtree, they are either removed or non-removable? Actually the condition for parent removal is that all children are removed. If any child is non-removable, it will never be removed, so parent can never be removed. So the parent removal candidate must have no non-removable nodes in its subtree (except possibly the root? but root is not in subtree unless parent is root). So basically, the parent removal candidate's entire subtree must consist only of nodes that are removable. Since we process depth order, the children will be considered before parent. So we can maintain a set of removed nodes, and when considering a node, we check if all its children are in removed set (or will be removed? but we can only decide based on current state). Since we process in depth order, by the time we process a node, all its descendants have already been decided. So we can simply check: all children of the node are in the removed set (i.e., we have already decided to remove them). That implies the entire subtree is removable and has been removed. But if a descendant is non-removable, then it would not be removed, and thus the condition fails. So we need to ensure that for each child, either the child is removable and we already removed it, or the child is non-removable but then we cannot remove parent. So the condition becomes: all children of the node are in the removed set. That implicitly ensures no non-removable nodes in subtree.\n\nBut we also need to ensure that required_symbols are not lost. Since we have already ensured no required symbols in subtree (by precomputation), that covers it.\n\nBut careful: a child could be removable but we might decide not to remove it because it's needed for something? The only reason not to remove a removable child is if it is required (i.e., symbol in required_set) or if it has non-removable children of its own. Those are already handled. So if a node is removable and its subtree has no required symbols and all its children are removable? Actually we need to ensure that for each child, either it is removed or it is non-removable? That's not sufficient; if a child is removable but its own subtree has required symbols, then the child cannot be removed. So to satisfy the condition for parent, we need that child is either removed (meaning we decided to remove it) or is non-removable. But if child is non-removable, parent can't be removed because child is retained. So the only viable path is that all children are removed. Therefore, the condition is: all children of node are already in removed set. That means the entire subtree is being removed. So the removal candidate must have all descendants removed.\n\nThus the algorithm: We iterate through nodes in depth order (descending). For each node, we check if it is a candidate (removable, not required, not root, and its subtree has no required symbols). But the depth order ensures children are processed first. So as we go, we decide to remove nodes. The condition for removal: all children are already removed. So we need to maintain a removed set. For each node, if it is candidate and all its children are in removed set, then we can remove it (add to removed set and record in removal order). If not, we skip it (it will remain retained). Then after processing all, the retained set is all nodes not in removed set.\n\nBut wait: what about nodes that are non-removable? They are never considered for removal, so they stay. Also, what about nodes that are removable but whose subtree contains required symbols? They will never be removed because they don't pass the first filter.\n\nSo the algorithm is deterministic.\n\nNow we need to implement solve(case) that returns {\"removed_node_ids\": list, \"retained_node_ids\": list}. remnant order: removed_node_ids is the order in which they were actually removed (the order we processed and removed). retained_node_ids: sorted list of all node ids that are not removed.\n\nWe need to pay attention to \"grammar_valid\" and \"interestingness_preserved\". The grammar_valid might be that the tree after removal must be a valid tree where every retained node's parent is also retained (since if parent removed, children must be removed too). Our algorithm ensures that: when we remove a node, we require all children removed, so no orphan children. So valid. Interestingness_preserved: we ensure required symbols remain, which seems to be the oracle.\n\nEverything seems consistent.\n\nNow we need to implement in Python.\n\nSteps:\n- Parse case: it's a dict with keys \"nodes\" (list) and \"required_symbols\" (list) and maybe \"_case_nonce\" but ignore.\n- Build node_map: dict id -> node.\n- Build children lists: for each node, if parent not None, add child to parent's children list.\n- Identify root: parent None.\n- Compute required_set = set(required_symbols)\n- Compute subtree_required_symbols for each node using DFS from leaves. Simple post-order: for each node, its set is union of its own symbol (if in required_set) and its children's sets.\n- Identify all node ids that are candidates: \n   - id != root (root can be any id, but root has parent None)\n   - node['removable'] is True (boolean)\n   - node['symbol'] not in required_set\n   - the subtree required set is empty (i.e., no required symbols in subtree)\n- Then create a list of candidate node ids sorted by depth descending, then by id string ascending (for tie break). We'll need the depth field from node.\n- Initialize removed_set = set()\n- removed_order = []\n- For each cand_id in sorted list:\n   - node = node_map[cand_id]\n   - check if all children of node are already in removed_set (we need children list). For root we skip anyway.\n   - If yes, then add cand_id to removed_set, append to removed_order.\n- After loop, retained_set = set(node_map.keys()) - removed_set\n- retained_sorted = sorted(retained_set) (lexicographically)\n- removed_order list (already in order)\n- Return {\"removed_node_ids\": removed_order, \"retained_node_ids\": retained_sorted}\n\nEdge cases: Some nodes may have no children, that's fine. The condition \"all children in removed_set\" is vacuously true for leaf nodes (empty list). So leaves that are candidates and have no required symbols in subtree (which they don't if they are not required symbols) will be removed first.\n\nBut there is a nuance: What about a node that is candidate but whose children are not all removable? For example, a node might have a child that is non-removable (removable=False, not required). That child will never be in removed_set. So the parent will never be removed because condition fails. That matches \"node with retained children\". So it stays.\n\nAlso, what about a node whose children are all removable, but one child's subtree contains a required symbol, so that child is not a candidate (its subtree has required). Then that child will not be removed. So parent's children set will have that child not in removed_set, so parent cannot be removed. That matches expectation.\n\nThis seems correct.\n\nNow we need to produce the implementation as a JSON string.\n\nImportant: The function `solve(case)` must not mutate case. We'll make a copy only if needed, but we are just reading.\n\nNow produce the code.\n\nWe'll use standard library only (json for output, but not needed because we return dict).\n\nWe'll write the function.\n\nTesting with the given fixtures: we can manually verify.\n\nFor a-000:\nnodes: root (0), n1(1, removable false), n2(1, removable true), n3(1, removable false), n4(2, removable true, parent n2), n5(2, removable false, parent n3), n6(2, removable true, parent n1).\nrequired_set = {'root','s1'}\nCompute subtree required:\n- n4: symbol s1, so {'s1'}\n- n2: children n4, so {'s1'}\n- n6: symbol s0, not required, no required in subtree (none)\n- n1: children n6, symbol s1, so {'s1'}\n- n5: symbol s2, not required, no required\n- n3: children n5, symbol s0, so no required\n- root: children n1,n2,n3, symbol root, so {'root','s1'}\n\nCandidates: nodes with removable true, not in required_set, subtree required empty:\n- n4: removable true, symbol s1 is required, so not candidate (symbol in required_set)\n- n2: removable true, subtree required non-empty -> not candidate\n- n6: removable true, subtree required empty, symbol s0 not required -> candidate\n- n1: removable false -> not candidate\n- n3: removable false -> not candidate\n- n5: removable false -> not candidate\n- root: not candidate (removable false and required)\nOnly n6 is candidate? Wait also n2? No. So only n6. But we expected n4 and n2 to be removed. However n4 is not candidate because its symbol is required, but it is removable=true. Yet we thought we could remove n4 because required symbol s1 is also present in n1. But our rule \"tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented\" means that even if the node itself has a required symbol, we can remove it if the required symbol also appears elsewhere? The problem statement: \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" It says \"a required symbol\", meaning the node itself whose symbol is required cannot be removed. But in the fixtures, n4 has symbol s1, which is required. So according to that rule, n4 cannot be removed. But in our analysis we thought we could remove n4 because s1 is also in n1. Let's read the rule again: \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" That implies any node whose symbol is in required_symbols cannot be removed. So n4 cannot be removed. That changes the outcome. Then only n6 and n2 can be removed? But n2's child n4 cannot be removed, so n2 has a retained child (n4), so n2 cannot be removed. So only n6 can be removed. That would yield removed_nodes = [\"n6\"], retained = all others. Is that plausible? Check the requirement: \"Reduce a registered parse tree through grammar-valid deletion and replacement candidates while preserving an interestingness oracle.\" If we cannot remove nodes that are required symbols, then the only removable ones are those with symbols not required. In that case, n6 is the only removable node with no required children (n6 has no children) and no non-removable children. So only n6 is removed. But is that consistent with the fixture? Possibly. The fixture may expect that. Let's cross-check with the expected output? Not provided. But we need to adhere to the rules.\n\nThe problem says \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" So \"required symbol\" means a node whose symbol is in required_symbols, not a node that is required for the set. So n4 (symbol s1) is required, cannot remove. So our earlier reasoning was wrong. That changes the algorithm.\n\nNow we need to adjust: a node is non-removable if its symbol is in required_symbols. So candidates are only those with removable true and symbol not in required_symbols. Also, root is non-removable. Then we process in depth order, and removal condition: all children are in removed set (i.e., already removed). That means we can only remove leaves that are not required symbols. Then, after removing a leaf, its parent may have no children left, so then the parent becomes a leaf? But the parent still has the tree structure. The condition \"node with retained children\" means if it currently has any child not removed, it cannot be removed. So after removing all children, the parent may become candidate for removal (if it is removable and its symbol not required). So we can remove n2 only after n4 is removed. But n4 cannot be removed because it's required. So n2 will never be removable because n4 remains. So n2 stays.\n\nThus for fixture a-000, only n6 can be removed. So removed_node_ids = [\"n6\"], retained sorted all others.\n\nCheck n5 is non-removable, so stays. n4 stays. So retained = [\"n1\",\"n2\",\"n3\",\"n4\",\"n5\",\"root\"] sorted = [\"n1\",\"n2\",\"n3\",\"n4\",\"n5\",\"root\"].\n\nBut then the tree still contains required symbols root and s1 (n1 and n4). That's fine.\n\nNow, we need to ensure that \"all required symbols remain represented\" after removal. It does.\n\nSo the algorithm is now: \n- Precomputation: root, nodes with symbol in required_set are non-removable.\n- For each node, we can compute if it is \"eligible_for_removal\" if removable true and symbol not in required_set and not root.\n- Then process nodes in depth descending order, and for each, if all children are removed, remove it.\n\nBut also we need to ensure that removing a node does not cause loss of required symbols. Since required symbols are always present in non-removable nodes, and those nodes are never removed, the set of required symbols is inherently preserved. So no extra check needed.\n\nTherefore, the algorithm is simpler.\n\nImplement: \n- Build map id->node, children list.\n- Identify candidate nodes: removable and symbol not in required_set and id != root_id.\n- Sort candidates by depth descending, then by id ascending.\n- removed_set = set()\n- removed_order = []\n- For cand_id in sorted_candidates:\n   - if all(child_id in removed_set for child_id in children[cand_id]):\n        removed_set.add(cand_id)\n        removed_order.append(cand_id)\n- retained = all_ids - removed_set\n- Sort retained lexicographically.\n- Return dict.\n\nThat should work.\n\nNow we need to produce the code as a string in a JSON object with key \"implementation\". The code must define `solve(case)` function.\n\nEdge Cases: The case may have nodes that are not in tree (orphans?) but all have parent or null. We assume input is valid.\n\nWrite the code.\n\nWe'll write:\n\ndef solve(case):\n    nodes = case['nodes']\n    required = set(case['required_symbols'])\n    node_map = {n['id']: n for n in nodes}\n    children = {n['id']: [] for n in nodes}\n    root_id = None\n    for n in nodes:\n        if n['parent'] is None:\n            root_id = n['id']\n        else:\n            children[n['parent']].append(n['id'])\n    # candidates\n    candidates = []\n    for n in nodes:\n        nid = n['id']\n        if nid == root_id:\n            continue\n        if n['symbol'] in required:\n            continue\n        if not n['removable']:\n            continue\n        candidates.append(nid)\n    # sort by depth desc, then id asc\n    candidates.sort(key=lambda x: (-node_map[x]['depth'], x))\n    removed_set = set()\n    removed_order = []\n    for cid in candidates:\n        if all(child in removed_set for child in children[cid]):\n            removed_set.add(cid)\n            removed_order.append(cid)\n    retained = sorted(set(node_map.keys()) - removed_set)\n    return {\"removed_node_ids\": removed_order, \"retained_node_ids\": retained}\n\nNote: The depth field is provided, so we can use it directly.\n\nWe must ensure we use standard library only. This is fine.\n\nNow produce the JSON output.\n\nThe response must be exactly one JSON object with key \"implementation\" and value a string containing the Python source. So we need to double-encode the string. We'll create a Python dict {\"implementation\": code_string} and then json.dumps it.\n\nBut the AI must output raw JSON. So we will write the JSON manually, ensuring proper escaping.\n\nLet's write the code as a string:\n\ndef solve(case):\\n    nodes = case['nodes']\\n    required = set(case['required_symbols'])\\n    node_map = {n['id']: n for n in nodes}\\n    children = {n['id']: [] for n in nodes}\\n    root_id = None\\n    for n in nodes:\\n        if n['parent'] is None:\\n            root_id = n['id']\\n        else:\\n            children[n['parent']].append(n['id'])\\n    candidates = []\\n    for n in nodes:\\n        nid = n['id']\\n        if nid == root_id:\\n            continue\\n        if n['symbol'] in required:\\n            continue\\n        if not n['removable']:\\n            continue\\n        candidates.append(nid)\\n    candidates.sort(key=lambda x: (-node_map[x]['depth'], x))\\n    removed_set = set()\\n    removed_order = []\\n    for cid in candidates:\\n        if all(child in removed_set for child in children[cid]):\\n            re